  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Weather Consistency's Relationship to Mental Health in the US\n",
    "\n",
    "# Permissions\n",
    "\n",
    "Place an `X` in the appropriate bracket below to specify if you would like your group's project to be made available to the public. (Note that student names will be included (but PIDs will be scraped from any groups who include their PIDs).\n",
    "\n",
    "* [  ] YES - make available\n",
    "* [ X ] NO - keep private\n",
    "\n",
    "# Names\n",
    "\n",
    "- Preity Singh\n",
    "- Lavin Ma\n",
    "- Katie Ngo\n",
    "- Thatcher Eames\n",
    "- Krrish Jain\n",
    "\n",
    "# Abstract\n",
    "\n",
    "This project investigates whether seasonal temperature variability plays a role in the differences in mental health outcomes across U.S. cities. We assessed the relationship between flunctuations in daily temperatures and rates of mental distress in 2021 and 2022.\n",
    "\n",
    "Throughout our analysis, we combined weather data from the Carnegie Mellon Climate Database with CDC's PLACES model-based mental health estimates. This was done through computing each city's average temperature, temperature standard deviation, and its range. Next, we visualized and incorporated our interpretations based on the patterns we discovered.\n",
    "\n",
    "Our analysis found no clear statistical relationship between temperature variability and mental distress. A linear regression model using 2021 weather data to predict 2022 mental health outcomes explained less than 2% of the variance. This shows that temperature variation alone is not a strong predictor of mental well-being and happiness. Our findings highlight that mental health is highly complex; there are a wide range of structural and social determinants that influence mental health."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Research Question"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Does seasonal temperature varibiability, which will be measured by the standard deviation from average daily temperatures, help explain differences in location-based mental health metrics, measured by the CDC in the years 2021 and 2022?\n",
    "Our research will attempt to minimize the influence of confounding variables by accounting for other  impactful statistics on mental health.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Subquestion:\n",
    "Which factor more closely aligns with mental health outcomes: average temperature or temperature variability?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Background and Prior Work"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "The relationship between weather and human well-being has been a topic of interest in environmental research for years, with strong connections to psychological studies. Numerous studies have shown that weather conditions such as sunny days tend to improve mood, while rainy days can increase feelings of sadness. Although these findings are valuable, weather is a complex phenomenon that encompasses a wide range of factors, making it challenging to grasp its direct effects on human well-being. To address this, our research aims to investigate the role of temperature variability within a single day. We will examine whether the fluctuation between daytime high and nighttime low temperatures has a profound effect mental health, as well as a city’s overall happiness. By focusing on temperature variability rather than climate trends, we can more accurately identify patterns that reveal how weather affects mental distress.\n",
    "\n",
    "Past research has utilized long-term average temperatures to draw conclusions about the impact of weather on happiness. For example, Rehdanz’s study of climate trends in 67 countries, using mean temperature and precipitation in the hottest and coldest months, found that higher temperatures during the coldest month were associated with greater happiness.<a name=\"cite_ref-1\"></a>[<sup>1</sup>](#cite_note-1) Another study by Tsutsui found that happiness was highest at a maximum temperature of 13.9 Celsius.<a name=\"cite_ref-2\"></a>[<sup>2</sup>](#cite_note-2) However, the results also indicated that although temperature influences people’s happiness, there is considerable variation in their responses. Few studies have explored how day-to-day weather changes, such as shifts between extreme and low temperatures, affect individuals’ happiness. A notable exception is research by Lucas and Lawless, which analyzed data from 1 million Americans over five years, combining different daily weather variables with life satisfaction ratings. They concluded that there was no significant correlation between temperature and life satisfaction judgments.<a name=\"cite_ref-3\"></a>[<sup>3</sup>](#cite_note-3)\n",
    "\n",
    "**Our work narrows the focus to temperature variability and its relationship with rates of mental distress. Past work has focused on average temperatures and its relationship to happiness. Does the overall consistency of the weather, consistently hot or cold rather than prone to large fluctutions throughout the year, correlate with overall happiness? Does stability bring increase mental well-being?**\n",
    "\n",
    "This question connects to a broader idea in psychology: the more uncontrolled factors in your life, the more likely they are to impact your mental health. Weather, as an external and often unpredictable force, may function as one of these factors. If we view stability as a source of comfort and predictability, then cities with more stable weather patterns might foster a greater sense of peace and happiness among residents. Constant change, even in something as mundane as temperature, could contribute to stress or discomfort. Does greater stability in the environment support mental well-being? This is the central question our project seeks to explore.\n",
    "\n",
    "1. <a name=\"cite_note-1\"></a> [^](#cite_ref-1) Rehdanz, Katrin (8 Dec 2004) Climate and Happiness. *Ecological Economics*. https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0921800904002940 \n",
    "2. <a name=\"cite_note-2\"></a> [^](#cite_ref-2) Tsutsui, Yoshiro (1 Jan 2013) Weather and Individual Happiness. *Weather, Climate, and Society*. https://journals.ametsoc.org/view/journals/wcas/5/1/wcas-d-11-00052_1.xml \n",
    "3. <a name=\"cite_note-3\"></a> [^](#cite_ref-3) Lucas, Richard E. and Lawless, Nicole M. (1 May 2014) Does Life Seem Better on a Sunny Day? *National Library of Medicine*. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3695376/"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Hypothesis\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "Our hypothesis is that cities with greater seasonal temperature variability, measured by the standard deviation of daily temperatures, will result in higher rates of depression and an increase in mental distress among adults.\n",
    "The thought process here centers on environmental stability, where predictable and consistent weather patterns play a role in psychological stability. Conversely, extreme shifts in temperature reduce our sense of control, disrupt daily routines, and raise stress levels, all of which negatively impact mental health."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Data"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Data overview\n",
    "\n",
    "For each dataset include the following information\n",
    "- Dataset #1\n",
    "  - Dataset Name: **CDC Mental Health Data**\n",
    "  - Link to the dataset: https://www.cdc.gov/places/about/index.html\n",
    "  - Number of observations: 3,592\n",
    "  - Number of variables: 7\n",
    "  - Description: The key variables include the location, population count and percentage, and health measure names. We will filter out the two measures: “Depression among adults” and “Frequent mental distress among adults” for our analysis of the years 2021 and 2022.\n",
    "- Dataset #2\n",
    "  - Dataset Name: **Carnegie Mellon Temperature and Precipitation Data**\n",
    "  - Link to the dataset: https://kilthub.cmu.edu/articles/dataset/Compiled_daily_temperature_and_precipitation_data_for_the_U_S_cities/7890488\n",
    "  - Number of observations: 238,710\n",
    "  - Number of variables: 8\n",
    "  - Description: This dataset contains daily weather data for each day starting earlier than year 1900 within 210 U.S. cities. The key variables in this dataset with their respective city names are max/min temperature in Fahrenheit (float), precipitation in inches (float), and data (string). We will use these metrics for daily climate variability by filtering for the years 2021 and 2022, extracting the appropriate month and day, and removing unnecessary columns.\n",
    "\n",
    "Combining the Datasets: In order to analyze the relationship between temperature variability and mental health outcomes, cities appearing in both datasets were selected for analysis. We matched city name and year to calculate temperature variability using the standard deviation of daily temperatures. Using comparative analysis, we integrated the data with mental health statistics to draw meaningful conclusions."
   ]
  },
  {
